WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.890
(pensive music)

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.150
- [Narrator] Russian Iskander missiles

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.180
like the ones seen in this
Belarusian state media footage,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.673
are capable of launching nuclear warheads.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.670
They've been popping up at
military training grounds

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.740
in Belarus as Russia aims to expand

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.453
its nuclear capabilities there.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.140
Iskanders are only manufactured in Russia.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.090
They're distinguishable
by the dual set of wheels

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.000
on the main chassis, and
this rectangular compartment

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.653
from which the missile launches.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.839
Russian President, Vladimir
Putin, has repeatedly boasted

00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:32.340
about the transfer of them to Belarus,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.140
ratcheting up the threat of
a nuclear attack from there.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.380
While it's difficult to know

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.260
if Russia has transferred
actual nuclear warheads,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.750
we can see that it's
expanding its capability

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.230
to launch them outside of
its borders in Belarus,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.930
right on Ukraine and NATO's doorstep.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.060
It's forced Ukraine to
grapple with questions

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.700
of a potential nuclear threat in the North

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:54.900
while trying to push forward

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.840
a major counteroffensive in the South.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.240
Russia has been building up
this capability in Belarus

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.260
since before the full scale
invasion of Ukraine in 2022.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.330
Much of it in the Asipovichy District,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.760
which has become a hub for
nuclear capable weapons

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.500
transferred from Russia.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.440
This January, 2022
satellite image from there

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.900
shows the beginning of this expansion.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.143
Here, over a dozen Iskander components

00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:20.643
are seen parked at a training center.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.750
Then in February of 2023,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.940
state media footage from the same location

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.230
shows Belarusian soldiers

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.780
training with the Russian
supplied Iskanders there.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.730
A few miles away from this
training ground in July, 2023

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.030
we can see a garage to store
some of the Russian launchers.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.733
The garage didn't exist in August, 2022.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.320
Elsewhere in the Asipovichy District,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.390
nuclear arms watchers began monitoring

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.570
another storage facility in spring of 2023

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.730
after a leaked US intelligence report

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.260
revealed Russia was considering it

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.033
to house nuclear warheads.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.520
Nuclear weapons researchers
have spotted clues

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.530
in satellite imagery there.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.160
Last summer, this area used
to be covered with trees.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.860
Only the outer perimeter had fencing.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.900
But by spring of 2023,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.670
these additional double layers of fencing

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.130
in the inner perimeter were added.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.920
While other features would
still need to be added,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.110
multiple layers of fencing are a signature

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.543
of Russian nuclear warhead storage sites.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.240
This one in Kaliningrad, Russia,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.043
has similar layers of fencing.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.090
The training grounds and
other facilities in Belarus

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.070
where Iskanders have been spotted,

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.650
along with the expansion
of storage arsenals

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.110
represent an important strategic expansion

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.943
of Russia's capability to
launch a nuclear weapon.

00:02:42.943 --> 00:02:45.610
(pensive music)

